1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,740
So, your marketing campaign assignment,
whose campaign did you choose?

2
00:00:04,109 --> 00:00:09,460
We almost went with BrightFit, but there
wasn't enough data, so we chose Northline.

3
00:00:09,890 --> 00:00:11,520
Northline makes a lot of things.

4
00:00:11,820 --> 00:00:17,020
Right, smartwatches, headphones, but this
campaign was just for their fitness app.

5
00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:18,540
Which channels did they use?

6
00:00:18,790 --> 00:00:21,512
They ran ads on
TikTok and YouTube, but

7
00:00:21,536 --> 00:00:24,832
Instagram got by far
the most likes and shares.

8
00:00:25,080 --> 00:00:26,080
And the impact?

9
00:00:26,270 --> 00:00:30,540
Downloads went up 20%, but the
more important figure is sign-ups.

10
00:00:30,980 --> 00:00:32,900
They rose 35%.

11
00:00:33,199 --> 00:00:34,240
What did it all cost?

12
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:40,180
They spent $40,000 on social media
alone, but the overall figure was $50,000.

13
00:00:40,989 --> 00:00:42,109
Who was the target audience?

14
00:00:42,429 --> 00:00:45,898
They aimed at 25 to
34-year-olds, but the group

15
00:00:45,922 --> 00:00:49,032
that actually responded
best was 18 to 24.

16
00:00:49,529 --> 00:00:51,440
Grace, where would you start the analysis?

17
00:00:51,880 --> 00:00:54,963
Before looking at sign-ups,
I'd look at the click-through

18
00:00:54,987 --> 00:00:57,432
rate, whether people
even clicked on the ads.

19
00:00:57,760 --> 00:00:58,605
Good.

20
00:00:58,629 --> 00:01:01,327
Students always measure
new customers and forget about

21
00:01:01,351 --> 00:01:04,212
customer retention, whether
people stay with the product.

22
00:01:04,479 --> 00:01:05,900
We've hardly mentioned that.

23
00:01:06,229 --> 00:01:07,469
How did Northline get feedback?

24
00:01:07,909 --> 00:01:09,380
They didn't use focus groups.

25
00:01:09,380 --> 00:01:12,820
Just an online survey emailed
to everyone who joined.

26
00:01:13,144 --> 00:01:17,020
72% were positive, and only
about 10% were negative.

27
00:01:17,529 --> 00:01:18,800
What did people complain about?

28
00:01:19,159 --> 00:01:22,540
A few mentioned bugs,
but mostly it was the price.

29
00:01:22,999 --> 00:01:25,360
People felt the subscription
was too expensive.

30
00:01:25,839 --> 00:01:27,199
Are you comparing it with a rival?

31
00:01:27,499 --> 00:01:29,580
We looked at FitPulse, but it's tiny.

32
00:01:30,114 --> 00:01:31,760
FitTrack is their main competitor.

33
00:01:32,229 --> 00:01:33,589
How are you crunching the numbers?

34
00:01:33,740 --> 00:01:34,760
A statistics package?

35
00:01:35,119 --> 00:01:38,420
We thought about one, but a
spreadsheet does everything we need.

36
00:01:38,769 --> 00:01:39,969
How have you divided the work?

37
00:01:40,189 --> 00:01:43,640
Grace has done the data, and I'm
writing the recommendations section.

38
00:01:43,939 --> 00:01:46,240
Grace, what's the key change you'd suggest?

39
00:01:46,579 --> 00:01:49,387
The advertising is
working. It's the pricing

40
00:01:49,411 --> 00:01:52,552
model that needs to
change. Maybe a cheaper tier.

41
00:01:52,989 --> 00:01:54,240
How long did the campaign run?

42
00:01:54,499 --> 00:01:58,240
It was planned for a month, but
they extended it. Six weeks in the end.

43
00:01:58,559 --> 00:01:59,720
Which channel did worst?

44
00:02:00,069 --> 00:02:04,480
Radio was poor, but the print adverts
gave the lowest return on investment.

45
00:02:04,609 --> 00:02:08,449
In your conclusion, don't just
celebrate the jump in sign-ups.

46
00:02:08,473 --> 00:02:11,352
Focus on the long-term
impact. When is it due?

47
00:02:11,640 --> 00:02:13,680
We thought Friday, but it's next Monday.

48
00:02:14,084 --> 00:02:17,040
And keep it to 2,000 words. Don't go over.

49
00:02:17,690 --> 00:02:21,600
We're at about 2,500, so we'll cut it down.

